use ../online/common

anchor(
  "functions",
  "### Functions"
);

let mdNatKeyword = x => "<span class=#{quote("codefont keyword")}>#{x}</span>";
let mdNatConstant = x => "<span class=#{quote("codefont constant")}>#{x}</span>";
let mdNatCode = x => "<span class=#{quote("codefont")}>#{x}</span>";

markdown "
  Nat is a simple language, consisting of functions and the things
  that functions act on. To define a function, introduce a variable and then
  do something with it after a fat arrow.
";

codeblock "let f = x => x + 1;";

markdown "Apply the function to an
  argument with parentheses and print the result with the #{mdNatKeyword("print")} function.
";

codeblock "print f(1);";

markdown "Functions can have any number of parameters. Wrapping *n* parameters in
parentheses and separating them with commas produces an *n*-ary function.";

codeblock "let f = (x, y, z) => x + y - z;

print f(1, 2, 3); ";

markdown "Omitting the parentheses and commas produces a curried function.";

codeblock "let f = x y z => x + y - z;

print f(1)(2)(3); ";

markdown "Affixing a parameter with a '#{mdNatCode("*")}' produces a variadic function. Within the scope
of the function, the parameter will be a sequence. We'll see sequences in more detail later.";

codeblock "let f = (*x) => x.reduce(+, 0);

print f(1);
print f(1, 2);
print f(1, 2, 3); ";

markdown "Functions can have zero parameters.";

codeblock "let f = () => 1;

print f();";

markdown "Functions can be partial. To define a partial function, use a value for a
parameter. The function will be undefined at any other value.";

codeblock "let f = 1 => 2;

print f(1);
print f(2); ";

markdown "Match multiple values by separating parameter image pairs with a '#{mdNatCode("|")}'.";

codeblock "let f = 1 => 2 | 2 => 3;

print f(1);
print f(2);
print f(3); ";

markdown "You can match specific values when defining total functions as well, by
supplying a final catchall parameter, which is just a normal bound variable.";

codeblock "let f = 1 => 2 | x => x * 2;

print f(1);
print f(2);";

markdown "Functions can be anonymous, and they can be the arguments of other functions.
Here we pass two anonymous functions to the built in composition function.";

codeblock "let f = (x => x + 1) . (x => x - 1);

print f(1);
print f(2); ";

markdown "While the default syntax for applying a function is with parentheses, it's also
possible to define prefix functions, which apply to arguments that they precede.
Prefix functions must be unary.";

codeblock "let prefix f = x => x + 1;

print f 1;";

markdown "The #{mdNatKeyword("print")} function is a prefix function that nat defines for you.";

codeblock "print print;";

markdown "You can define infix functions in a similar fashion. Infix functions must
be binary.";

codeblock "let infix plus = (x, y) => x + y;

print 2 plus 2;";

markdown "Note that both prefix and infix functions can be called with parentheses.";

codeblock "print(plus(2,2);";

markdown "The arithmetic operators that we have been using so far are infix functions
defined by the interpreter.";

codeblock "[+,-,*].map(print);";

markdown "Two paradigms are available when defining the body of a function: the functional
and the imperative. So far we have used the functional paradigm, in which the entire body
is an expression, and the function implicitly returns that expression when evaluated.

<br>

The imperative paradigm allows a sequence of statements to be evaluated and makes a return value
optional.";

codeblock "let f = x => {
  x = x + 1;
  print x;
};

f(1);";

markdown "If an imperative function has no explicit return value, it will return #{mdNatCode("nil")} when called.";

codeblock "print(f(1));";

markdown "To return a value from a function, use the #{mdNatKeyword("return")} keyword.";

codeblock "let f = x => {
  return f;
};";

markdown "The body of an imperative function is its own namespace, so you can define variables with
the #{mdNatKeyword("let")} keyword, just as in the top level of these blocks of code. In fact, each
of these blocks, and any file written in nat, is itself an imperative function.";

anchor(
  "values",
  "### Values"
);

markdown "
  Certain primitive objects are always available: numbers, truth values, strings, and symbols.
  Alongside these objects are basic functions for doing arithmetic, boolean
  logic, string manipulation, and for testing equality.
  
  <br>

  Numbers are created just as we've been doing, and decimals are similarly intuitive. The arithmetic operations
  defined by the interpreter are addition #{mdNatCode("+")}, subtraction #{mdNatCode("-")},
  division #{mdNatCode("/")}, multiplication #{mdNatCode("*")}, and greater than #{mdNatCode(">")}, less than #{mdNatCode("<")},
  greater than or equal #{mdNatCode(">=")}, and less than or equal #{mdNatCode("<=")}.
  
  <br>

  The boolean literals are #{mdNatConstant("true")} and #{mdNatConstant("false")}.
";

anchor(
  "objects",
  "### Objects"
);

markdown "
Nat defines a number of objects for structuring
other objects: sets, sequences, maps, and trees.

<br>

#### Sets
<br>
To make a set, use curly braces.
";

codeblock "let x = {1, 2, 3};";

markdown "The set object comes with some familiar functions,
which you can access with the #{mdNatCode("object.function")} syntax.";

codeblock "let x = {1, 2, 3}.union({3, 4, 5});
print x;";

markdown "Some functions have terser symbolic aliases that can be used as infixes.";

codeblock "let y = {1, 2, 3} & {3, 4, 5};
print x == y;";

markdown "Set objects also have unary properties.";

codeblock "print {1, 2, 3}.powerset();";

markdown "It's possible to make a set by specifying
conditions that its elements satisfy. We call this a *set comprehension*.";

codeblock "print {a | a in {true,false}, a};";

markdown "When using the comprehension syntax, we always introduce a bound
variable and a source object with a #{mdNatCode("variable")} #{mdNatKeyword(" in ")} #{mdNatCode("object")} phrase.
These are the generators for the comprehensions. Once a variable has been
introduced, we can restrict it with any number of predicates to its right. We can
also refer to it within other generators to its right.";

codeblock "print {b | a in {{1, 2}, {3, 4}}, b in a, b not in {2, 3}};";

markdown "While they're not the same as functions, sets can be applied to arguments,
so that in certain situations they're indistinguishable from their characteristic functions.";

codeblock "let x = {1, 2, 3};
print x(1);
print x(4);";

markdown "Arbitrary expressions can occur in the body of set comprehensions, including
other set comprehensions. (There is a caveat &ndash; such expressions must be hashable. We will
gloss over this detail for the moment.)";

codeblock "print {{y | y in {x + 1, x - 1}} | x in {1,2,3}};";

markdown "
#### Sequences
<br>

A sequence is a totally ordered multiset. To make a sequence, use square brackets.";

codeblock "let x = [1, 2, 3];
print x;";

markdown "Because sequences have order, they can be accessed by index. Indices begin at 0.";

codeblock "print [1, 2, 3][0];";

markdown "
#### Maps
<br>
To define a map, enclose a sequence of key value pairs, separated by colons, within curly braces.
Access the value at some key with square brackets.
";

codeblock "let x = {true: false, false: true};

print x[true];
print x[false];";

markdown "
#### Trees

<br>

To define a tree, use square brackets, and separate the nodes with spaces.

<br>

(Note that to visualize
trees, we will typeset them in LaTeX, but because we rely on a remote server
for the typesetting, this will often not be instantaneous.)
";

codeblock "let x = [1 2 3];

print x;";

markdown "Specify the value of an interior node by affixing it to its leftmost bracket
with a '#{mdNatCode(".")}'.";

codeblock "let x = [.1 2 3];

print x;";

markdown "Arbitrary expressions can occur as the data for trees.";

codeblock "let f = x => x + 1;

print [.f(1) f(2) f(2)];";

markdown "However, because we use whitespace to distinguish nodes, any expression that
itself contains whitespace must be wrapped in parentheses.";

codeblock "print [.(1 + 1) (1 + 2) (1 + 3)];";

markdown "Access subtrees by index.";

codeblock "let x = [.0 [.2 3 4] [.5 6 7]];

print x;
print x[0];
print x[1];";

markdown "Access tree data via the #{mdNatCode("tree.data")} property.";

codeblock "let x = [.1 2 3];

print x.data;
print x[0].data;
print x[1].data;";

anchor(
  "control",
  "### Control"
);

markdown "Nat has operators for conditional and iterative computations.

<br>

#### Conditionals

<br>

To conditionally execute a statement, check if some condition holds.";

codeblock "if (true) print 1;";

markdown "Optionally specify an alternative condition if the initial condition fails.";

codeblock "if (true) print 1; else if (false) print 2;";

markdown "Include any number of #{mdNatKeyword("if else")} clauses, and specify what to
do if no condition holds with a final #{mdNatKeyword("else")} clause.";

codeblock "let f = x => {
  if (x == 0) print 0;
  else if (x == 1) print 1;
  else if (x == 2) print 2;
  else print 3;
};

f(0);
f(1);
f(2);
f(3);
f(4);";

markdown "Note that conditionals are *statements*, so they can only occur in
an imperative context.";

markdown "
#### Loops
<br>

To iterate over elements of an iterable object #{mdNatCode("y")}, use a
#{mdNatKeyword("for ")} #{mdNatCode("(x")} #{mdNatKeyword(" in ")} #{mdNatCode("y)")} phrase.
This will bind #{mdNatCode("x")} to each element, successively, in the context
of the following block of code.";

codeblock "for (x in [1,2,3]) print x;";


